    "description": "Job Description: School Counsellor, Haile-Manas Academy Haile-Manas Academy (HMA) is a model, first-of-its-kind co-educational boarding school, established for the education of Ethiopia’s most promising and motivated secondary students. The school provides its students a world-class education and strives to help transform the education available to all Ethiopian students. HMA is located approximately three hours outside of Addis Ababa, in Debre Birhan, Ethiopia, on a beautiful, state-of-the-art campus, and is now in its sixth year of operation. HMA recruits students from every regional state in Ethiopia and admits them regardless of financial circumstances, as far as possible. It is the flagship project of Ethiopia Education Initiatives, a US-based not-for-profit. The vast majority of HMA students leave home for the first time to join the school as Grade 9 students. Student wellbeing is the foundation of everything they achieve here. HMA seeks a dedicated, experienced professional to fulfill the role of School Counsellor. The School Counsellor helps HMA students grow into confident, resilient and caring young people. The role has three parts: Counselling students through group sessions and one-to-one support, the main focus of the role. Developing Teaching Faculty Skills so they can better understand and support students. Overseeing the Advisory programme , the school's regular lessons on personal, social and emotional development. The role of School Counsellor is a live-in role enabling the counsellor to be a part of the daily boarding life and present when students most often need support: in the evenings and at weekends. Key responsibilities Group counselling Plans and leads regular small-group sessions on topics such as homesickness, stress and exam anxiety, friendships, conflict resolution, self-confidence, grief, trauma, and healthy relationship building, etc. Runs specific workshops for new Grade 9, 10, 11 students adjusting to boarding life, and for Grade 12 students preparing to leave school. Uses student needs, teacher referrals and wellbeing surveys to decide which programs and groups to target each term. Creates a safe, respectful space where students learn from each other and build lasting peer support. Individual counselling Provides confidential one-to-one counselling for students facing personal, emotional, social or family challenges. Responds to students in distress, including in the evenings and at weekends, and follows the school's crisis procedures. Refers students, when necessary, to external psychological and/or medical professional services, in consultation with the School Administration, remaining involved in their care, where appropriate. Keeps accurate, confidential counselling records in line with school policy and professional ethics. Developing Teaching Faculty and House Parent skills Leads ongoing professional development for teachers on understanding adolescent development, wellbeing and student behaviour. Trains teachers in practical strategies for managing classrooms with care and supporting struggling students. Helps teachers recognize early signs of emotional distress and know when and how to refer a student. Offers one-to-one coaching to teachers and house parents on specific students or situations. Shares simple guides and resources so good practice becomes part of everyday school life. Advisory Programme Oversees the school's Advisory programme across Grades 9–12, including its goals, curriculum and lesson materials. Designs a clear sequence of Advisory lessons for each grade, covering topics such as self-awareness, emotional regulation, healthy relationships, decision-making and leadership. Prepares and supports teachers who deliver Advisory lessons, including training at the start of each year. Visits Advisory sessions, gathers feedback from students and teachers, and improves the programme each year. Boarding life and student wellbeing Works closely with house parents to support students' wellbeing in the residences. Visible and approachable in boarding life, joining evening and weekend activities on a regular basis. Helps plan school-wide wellbeing activities, such as orientation for new students, and peer-support program. In collaboration with School Administration, communicates with and supports families in a clear, compassionate, and sensitive manner when a student is experiencing challenges. Safeguarding Always follows the school’s safeguarding and child protection policy. Reports safeguarding concerns to Administration without delay. Helps review and improve the school's wellbeing and counselling procedures.",
