    "hoursAgo": 23,
    "datePosted": "2026-11-24T16:05:21.000Z",
    "description": "In partnership with the World Health Organization, Ethiopia’s Federal Ministry of Health (FMoH), and the Ethiopian Public Health Institute (EPHI), Resolve to Save Lives (RTSL) has been implementing Strengthening IDSR in Private Health Facilities. The project supports private health facilities to prevent, detect, and respond to public health threats while maintaining essential health services through core interventions of capacity building, longitudinal mentorship, supportive supervision, monitoring of key indicators, and data use to guide tailored interventions at the facility level. Length of Engagement: 4 months. The contracting and commencement of the assignment are contingent on confirmation and availability of funding. Level of Effort: Equivalent to FTE Location: Assigned region of work. The regional consultants will be deployed through the following clustered approach: Southwest Ethiopia, Central Ethiopia, Sidama, and South Ethiopia Amhara, Tigray, and Afar Benishangul-Gumuz, Gambella, and Oromia Harari, Dire Dawa, and Somali RTSL will cover travel costs, provided they are pre-approved by the contract manager and follow RTSL travel guidelines. Job Structure: Independent Contractor Must have a registered business license to operate as a consultant in Ethiopia, or be willing to get one before the contract is signed. Position Summary This consultancy will focus on expanding the implementation into additional facilities and strengthening the ongoing implementation of the project in 13 regions. The Consultant will coordinate the selection and assessment of facilities, training of mentors and mentees (facility focal points), and support the execution of regular mentorship to make the facilities ready to safely prevent, detect, and respond to public health emergencies while maintaining essential healthcare services. Five consultants are planned for this assignment: four regional implementation consultants and one data-management Consultant who will support implementation across all regions. Regional assignments will be confirmed based on project needs, consultant suitability, language and contextual considerations, and feasibility. Any subsequent reassignment to another region or group of facilities will be discussed and mutually agreed with the Consultant. The consultants will work closely with the Principal Manager for Prevent Epidemics and the Epidemic Ready Primary Health Care (ERPHC) Technical Advisor to achieve all deliverables. Core Tasks and Activities Develop concept notes that outline the delivery approach and plans for the specific regions assigned to each Consultant by the ERPHC Technical Advisor. Work closely with the assigned regions’ health bureau/regional public health institute and EPHI to identify private facilities to enroll in the project. Train assessors on RTSL’s capacity assessment tools and approach, support the data collection process, and analyze each region’s health facility data. In collaboration with regional PHI/RHB, support the selection of mentors and mentees and facilitate their training in line with the RTSL training package. Support the mentorship process to enable enrolled private health facilities to achieve significant improvement from their respective baseline scores on PHEM and IPC indicators. Review the mentorship data submitted by mentors each month and give mentors feedback after each mentorship week so that course correction can be done promptly Conduct data-quality spot checks and supportive supervisions at selected facilities, as needed, to verify that mentorship data submitted by mentors matches facility records. Organize performance review meetings to track improvements and share lessons learned. Support national-level advocacy to strengthen the preparedness and readiness of private health facilities for public health emergencies. Perform additional activities within the scope of and relevant to the project as agreed with the ERPHC Technical Advisor. Where project needs may require reassignment to another region or group of facilities, the proposed reassignment will be discussed and mutually agreed with the Consultant, taking into account feasibility and implementation requirements. Contract Deliverables Facility and mentor/mentee selection completed in the assigned region, and mentors and mentees (facility IPC/PHEM focal points) trained according to a phased implementation schedule agreed during onboarding. Baseline capacity assessments completed for enrolled facilities, followed by implementation of structured mentorship and collection, analysis, and visualization of mentorship data using RTSL tools. Monthly mentorship follow-up completed, including monitoring continuity of mentorship, data submission, and reporting, with feedback provided to mentors as needed. Regional engagement and buy-in secured from the respective Regional Public Health Institutes (RPHIs) and/or Regional Health Bureaus (RHBs) to support project implementation and sustainability. Endline assessment is completed. Significant improvement gains from baseline are achieved for all facilities in the overall PHEM and IPC scores. A 20% gain is ideal. The Independent Contractor will prepare and submit monthly progress reports summarizing implementation progress, mentorship performance, facility improvements, challenges, corrective actions, and next steps. Contract Management The Consultant will submit deliverables to the ERPHC Technical Advisor for review and acceptance. The Principal Manager for Prevent Epidemics will manage the contract and approve invoices.",
